z against Republican Eric Flores, with Libertarian and Green candidates also on the ballot. The seat sits in territory that has shifted right in recent cycles, and the district's partisan lean favors Republicans by about three points, making this one of the cycle's most competitive House races.\n\nGonzalez holds a clear financial edge and has emphasized water projects, energy jobs, and local issues, while Flores has raised substantial sums and drawn national Republican support, including a rally headlined by JD Vance. Most expert ratings land on Lean D, though two call it a Tossup, and markets give Gonzalez roughly an 83 percent chance. Polling is sparse and somewhat dated, showing a narrow Democratic lead, so the race is best described as Lean D with meaningful uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7969, "margin": 4.84}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7826, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7895, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8125, "margin": 5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7373, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7369, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7694, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.7877, "margin": 4.5}]}